<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the problem of sampling from the probability measure
<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathrm{d}x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathrm{d}x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in C^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in C^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies, for <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha\leq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha\leq\beta</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V(x)\preceq\beta I\,,\hskip 20.00003ptx\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\kappa\coloneqq\tfrac{\beta}{\alpha}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V(x)\preceq\beta I\,,\hskip 20.00003ptx\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\kappa\coloneqq\tfrac{\beta}{\alpha}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The main result of this paper is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_unnumberedtheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Main theorem; informal)<span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), and suppose that the initial law <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies the warm start condition in order-<math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> Rényi divergence: <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For every
<math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a randomized algorithm that, given
<math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and query access to <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V</annotation></semantics></math>, returns
<math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}\sim\widehat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}\sim\widehat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TV}(\widehat{\mu},\mu)\leq\varepsilon\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TV}(\widehat{\mu},\mu)\leq\varepsilon\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and whose expected number of queries to <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V</annotation></semantics></math> is at most</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}\bigl(\sqrt{\kappa}\,d^{1/4}\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}\bigl(\sqrt{\kappa}\,d^{1/4}\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}</annotation></semantics></math> hides universal powers of logarithms in
<math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmunnumberedtheoremx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The precise statement and parameter choices appear in
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Sampler from an order-2 Rényi warm start). ‣ Choice of parameters. ‣ 4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 4.4 (Optimized gradient complexity). ‣ Choice of parameters. ‣ 4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our result with <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-accuracy samplers</em>, that is,
samplers whose complexity depends polylogarithmically on
<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Under (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), the state of the art
until recently was the Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm
(MALA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>
and the proximal sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which achieved
<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa d^{1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa d^{1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> complexity.
Very recently, Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> used the first-order
rejection sampling (FORS) mechanism to develop a method for exact
simulation of diffusions. Together with accelerated entropic
hypocoercivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, this yielded
a high-accuracy sampler with complexity
<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{2/3}d^{1/3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{2/3}d^{1/3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, Lu and Luo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> introduced windowed thinning,
an exact simulation method for the bouncy particle sampler (BPS) and the zigzag sampler.
Their estimates give
expected complexities of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d)</annotation></semantics></math> gradient queries
for BPS and <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa d^{5/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa d^{5/4})</annotation></semantics></math> partial derivative queries
for zigzag, from a warm start.
These results were significant in part because they constituted progress
toward acceleration for log-concave sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From a warm start, our
main theorem achieves complexity
<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})</annotation></semantics></math>. It matches the condition number
dependence of BPS while improving its dimension
dependence, and always improves upon the computational cost of the zigzag sampler (even if we consider one gradient query to be <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> times as expensive as a partial derivative query).
It also improves over all other prior results in both parameters. In particular, it achieves full
acceleration together with <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence on dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In a companion work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, we will show how to obtain a warm start using
<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6})</annotation></semantics></math> gradient queries. We do not discuss the warm start
question further in this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our algorithm, the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler</em> (Proximal
BPS), combines two ideas. The proximal sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>
breaks the sampling problem into a sequence of easier local problems
by introducing a Gaussian perturbation of the current position.
The bouncy particle sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> explores a distribution
by moving a particle along straight lines, with random changes of direction
chosen to preserve the desired distribution. We bring these ideas
together by designing local particle motions that retain a memory of
previous steps. This memory acts as momentum, allowing the algorithm
to make sustained progress across successive local problems and giving
the accelerated convergence rate in our main theorem. The local motions
follow explicitly computable curved paths, with occasional “bounces”
that correct for the shape of the target distribution. A gradient
evaluation at a nearby reference point makes these corrections inexpensive:
since the gradient varies little over a small region, only a small
number of bounces is needed. Balancing the size of the local problems
against the cost of these corrections yields the
<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})</annotation></semantics></math> query bound. We feel that the construction of the sampler (with the help of GPT-5.6 Sol) contains some surprising ingredients.
We describe the construction and its geometry in
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Overview of the construction ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, and give the implementation and complexity
analysis in <a href="#S4" title="4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related work.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work makes progress on the complexity of log-concave sampling, for which the textbook <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> provides an introduction and overview.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proximal sampling framework is from Lee, Shen, and
Tian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, with subsequent analyses and implementations
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. These methods
alternate independent draws of the position and the auxiliary variable
from their conditional distributions. Our update reflects the auxiliary
point through the current position, a step related to the Gaussian
overrelaxation method of Neal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. We combine
this reflection with a local piecewise deterministic particle motion run for half of its
oscillation period. As we shall explain, this helps useful information to persist across iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The rule for the bounces builds on the BPS of Bouchard-Côté,
Vollmer, and Doucet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The use of a quadratic
reference potential to generate curved paths, with bounces correcting
for the remaining force, is adapted from the boomerang sampler of
Bierkens, Grazzi, Kamatani, and Roberts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. This separation
of the force also fits the general framework for Hamiltonian piecewise
deterministic Markov processes of Andrieu, Durmus, Nüsken, and
Roussel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing convergence analyses of these processes
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> concern continuous-time dynamics
with repeated random refreshment of the velocity. Our local motion has
no such refreshment: it stops after a half-period, and convergence
comes from combining it with the auxiliary reflection step with occasional
resampling. In particular, the local motion alone need not converge to
its conditional equilibrium. Thus, the continuous-time results do not
directly give a convergence guarantee for our discrete chain.
Our analysis instead adapts the modified <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> hypocoercivity method of
Dolbeault, Mouhot, and Schmeiser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, in the sharpened formulation
of Fan, Li, and Lu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, to this discrete setting. The technical details of the hypocoercivity mechanism in Appendices <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> should be of interest to specialists.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">AI usage.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm and its original analysis were found by interactions with GPT-5.6 Sol. The authors recast the arguments into a more standard hypocoercive framework, prepared the manuscript, and take full responsibility for its contents.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our algorithm operates on the augmented distribution underlying the
proximal sampler of Lee, Shen, and Tian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For a
scale <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1/\beta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1/\beta]</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the joint law</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The original proximal sampler algorithm performs Gibbs sampling on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Namely, under <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> one may generate the joint state as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mu\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\ \text{independently}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>independently</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mu\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\ \text{independently}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Its <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-marginal is <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>X</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> for
the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>-marginal, so that
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\propto e^{-U_{\eta}(y)}\,\mathrm{d}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\propto e^{-U_{\eta}(y)}\,\mathrm{d}y</annotation></semantics></math>; by
(<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 2.7 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>), <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}=\mu*\mathcal{N}(0,\eta I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}=\mu*\mathcal{N}(0,\eta I)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a Gaussian smoothing of the target.
We use superscripts for marginal and conditional laws; in particular,</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y\mid X=x}=\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y\mid X=x}=\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Gibbs sampling alternates exact draws from the two conditional laws in
(<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 2.8 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>).
The scale <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of the quadratic coupling
potential.
Decreasing <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> more tightly coupled and the
conditional law of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> more localized and better
conditioned, which reduces the cost of conditional sampling. The
resulting transitions are also more local, however, and generally require
more Gibbs sampling iterations. Thus <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> balances the cost of each
iteration against the number of iterations.
The convergence and query
complexity are studied further in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The next proposition collects the basic geometric properties of the
augmented distribution used throughout the paper: the curvature and
concentration of the conditional law, the curvature of the
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>-marginal, and a reflection symmetry of the joint law.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Geometry of the joint law)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The joint law (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 2.6 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>) has the following properties.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.2em;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the conditional law
</span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has potential (that is, negative
log-density up to an additive constant)</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This conditional potential satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha+\eta^{-1})I\preceq\nabla^{2}V_{y}(x)\preceq(\beta+\eta^{-1})I\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha+\eta^{-1})I\preceq\nabla^{2}V_{y}(x)\preceq(\beta+\eta^{-1})I\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so its condition number is at most
</span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\beta\eta)/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\beta\eta)/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Consequently, for every
smooth </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(f)\leq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}\|\nabla f\|^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(f)\leq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}\|\nabla f\|^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\eta}{1+\beta\eta}I\preceq\mathrm{Cov}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(X)\preceq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}I\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\eta}{1+\beta\eta}I\preceq\mathrm{Cov}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(X)\preceq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}I\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-marginal potential satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha\eta}I\preceq\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)\preceq\frac{\beta}{1+\beta\eta}I\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha\eta}I\preceq\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)\preceq\frac{\beta}{1+\beta\eta}I\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The state-space reflection</span></p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}:(x,y)\mapsto(x,2x-y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}:(x,y)\mapsto(x,2x-y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">preserves </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and is an involution.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Hessian
bound in part (i) follows immediately from (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>); the
Poincaré and covariance bounds then follow from the standard
log-concavity facts (<a href="#A4.E6" title="Equation D.6 ‣ D.2 Strong log-concavity and covariance bounds ‣ Appendix D Technical tools ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.6</span></a>) and
<a href="#A4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma D.3 (Two-sided covariance bound). ‣ D.2 Strong log-concavity and covariance bounds ‣ Appendix D Technical tools ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Technical tools ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, applied with
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\alpha+\eta^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\alpha+\eta^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\beta+\eta^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\beta+\eta^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
marginal curvature bound in part (ii) is derived in
Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Technical tools ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, at (<a href="#A4.E10" title="Equation D.10 ‣ Hessian of the augmented marginal. ‣ D.2 Strong log-concavity and covariance bounds ‣ Appendix D Technical tools ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.10</span></a>). The
reflection statement follows directly from (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 2.6 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use the standard proximal map convention of
Moreau <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y)\coloneqq\arg\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\Bigl\{V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y)\coloneqq\arg\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\Bigl\{V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The minimizer is unique under (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>). The objective
minimized here is exactly the conditional potential
(<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 2.9 ‣ Item (i) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>), so <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the
mode of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math>; the conditional draw is in this
sense a stochastic analogue of a proximal step, whence the
terminology arises. This exact minimizer is used below only in the analysis; the implementable algorithm never evaluates it and
does not assume access to a proximal oracle.
We use the following
conditional sampling primitive, introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Restricted Gaussian oracle)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a restricted Gaussian oracle (RGO) takes input
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and, using fresh randomness, returns a sample with law
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Operator framework and micro–macro decomposition</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our target distribution is the joint law <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in
(<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 2.6 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>), and we set <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}\coloneqq L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}\coloneqq L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) that the ideal Proximal BPS has
transition kernel</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)=\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.150em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)=\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional half-turn kernel from
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel). ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Since the algorithm is a discrete-time
chain, its evolution is described by the induced one-step Markov operator
on observables,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on probability laws, whereas <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is its
induced action on observables. The first term in brackets
in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional resampling branch. After the
auxiliary coordinate is updated to <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this branch replaces the <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate by an independent draw from
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated conditional expectation operator
is</p>
<table id="A2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int f(x^{\prime},y)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int f(x^{\prime},y)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}f</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the coordinate <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The range
and kernel of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="A2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\text{ for some }g\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})\}\,,\\
\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\ker(\mathsf{P})=\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P}^{\perp})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℌ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.300em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℌ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.022em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\text{ for some }g\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})\}\,,\\
\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\ker(\mathsf{P})=\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P}^{\perp})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\mathsf{P}f+\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\mathsf{P}f+\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f</annotation></semantics></math>. In the common terminology of
hypocoercivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> is the
<em id="A2.SS1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">macroscopic</em> subspace and its orthogonal complement is the
<em id="A2.SS1.p1.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">microscopic</em> subspace.
Thus functions in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> depend only on the coordinate <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>,
whereas functions in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> have zero mean under
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-almost every <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Relative to the decomposition
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write an operator
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℌ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in block form as</p>
<table id="A2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\begin{aligned} \mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,,\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,.\end{aligned}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝖯𝖳𝖯</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>𝖯𝖳𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖳𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\begin{aligned} \mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,,\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,.\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The first subscript specifies the target subspace and the second the source
subspace; thus <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{ab}:\mathfrak{H}_{b}\to\mathfrak{H}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{ab}:\mathfrak{H}_{b}\to\mathfrak{H}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\{\mathsf{P},\perp\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\{\mathsf{P},\perp\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first apply this block notation to the reflection map <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> from
(<a href="#S2.E14" title="Equation 2.14 ‣ Item (iii) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>). We use the same symbol for its pullback on
observables:</p>
<table id="A2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\coloneqq f(\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\coloneqq f(\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This pullback is self-adjoint and unitary, and hence has block matrix</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯𝖴𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself, whereas
<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since
<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is self-adjoint and unitary, <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a self-adjoint
contraction on <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Writing <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}^{2}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖴</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}^{2}=I</annotation></semantics></math> in blocks gives</p>
<table id="A2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We next express the joint half-turn operator <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same block
decomposition. It is defined in (<a href="#A1.E9" title="Equation A.9 ‣ Proposition A.2 (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator). ‣ A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>) and is a
self-adjoint Markov contraction by
<a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition A.2 (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator). ‣ A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>. Since <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> acts only on the
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate, while every
function in <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖧𝖯</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Self-adjointness also gives
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}:\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\to\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖧𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}:\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\to\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The identity <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant under
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is its restriction to <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is
therefore self-adjoint, and
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}g\|=\|\mathsf{H}g\|\leq\|g\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}g\|=\|\mathsf{H}g\|\leq\|g\|</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt-I\preceq\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\preceq I\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝖧</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.778em">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝖧</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⪯</mo><mi>I</mi><mtext>on </mtext><mi>ℌ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt-I\preceq\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\preceq I\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Relative to <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>, this decomposition
has the block representation</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The Markov operator <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the transition kernel <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
therefore factors as</p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\rho\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}\bigr]=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\rho\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}\bigr]=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, its block representation is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">The diagonal factor in the block representation of <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> contracts
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> but leaves <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged. For an observable
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, contraction therefore relies on the
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>-component <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> created by reflection. The next
subsection estimates this component and the subsequent action of
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the reflection operator <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)=f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)=f(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> initially depends only on the auxiliary
coordinate <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, reflection gives <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which generally depends on both coordinates. Its macroscopic and microscopic
components are <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The following
probabilistic representation identifies the squared norm of the microscopic
component created by reflection with an expected conditional variance, thereby
enabling quantitative estimate by the conditional Poincaré estimates used in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, under <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}\coloneqq X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY_{-}\coloneqq X-\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX\sim\mu\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}\coloneqq X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY_{-}\coloneqq X-\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX\sim\mu\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y_{+},Y_{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y_{+},Y_{-})</annotation></semantics></math> is exchangeable. We henceforth identify
<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for a
macroscopic observable. Under this identification,</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)(y)=\mathbb{E}[f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)(y)=\mathbb{E}[f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the microscopic component <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> created when reflection
acts on <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#A2.E5" title="Equation B.5 ‣ B.1 Operator framework and micro–macro decomposition ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>),
<math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|\,,\hskip 10.00002ptf\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|\,,\hskip 10.00002ptf\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Thus a lower bound for <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how much of a macroscopic
observable reflection transfers into the microscopic subspace.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform macroscopic coercivity)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\eta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\eta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and set</span></p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\rm mac}\coloneqq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\gamma_{\rm gap}\coloneqq\sqrt{1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2}}=\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>gap</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\rm mac}\coloneqq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\gamma_{\rm gap}\coloneqq\sqrt{1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2}}=\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\rm gap}\geq\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\alpha\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>gap</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\rm gap}\geq\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\alpha\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the following hold.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(1) For every <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝖴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝖴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝖴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(2) Consider the mean-zero macroscopic subspace <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\coloneqq\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\cap L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\coloneqq\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\cap L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\|\leq\rho_{\rm mac}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{and}\hskip 10.00002pt\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I\hskip 20.00003pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\|\leq\rho_{\rm mac}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{and}\hskip 10.00002pt\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I\hskip 20.00003pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}=(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\succeq\gamma_{\rm gap}I\hskip 20.00003pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>gap</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}=(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\succeq\gamma_{\rm gap}I\hskip 20.00003pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>. The identity
(<a href="#A2.E9" title="Equation B.9 ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>) expresses <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the
conditional variance of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Y_{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Y_{-})</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Differentiating the
corresponding conditional expectation shows that this variance controls how
quickly <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> changes with <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The Poincaré inequality for
<math id="A2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> then bounds the norm of a centered <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of
<math id="A2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|</annotation></semantics></math>, giving the stated estimate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is boundedly invertible on <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
(<a href="#A2.E11" title="Equation B.11 ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.11</span></a>) gives the polar decomposition</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\,:\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\longrightarrow\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\,:\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\longrightarrow\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the size of the microscopic component created by
reflection, while the isometry <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> records its direction in
<math id="A2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The next proposition says that the half-turn acts nearly as <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-I</annotation></semantics></math> on the
corresponding microscopic directions <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Global half-turn bound)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist universal constants <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, if
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\eta\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\eta\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\bigr\|^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptv\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>gap</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\bigr\|^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptv\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition B.2 (Global half-turn bound). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> in
<a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 The half-turn operator on affine functions ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a> and <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Proof of : From affine estimates to the half-turn bound ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Together, <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemmas</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> and <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition B.2 (Global half-turn bound). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>
provide the estimates used in the modified <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> argument below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Proof of
<a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first prove
(<a href="#A2.E13" title="Equation B.13 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>). It suffices to consider
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the general case following by density and
closedness of the gradient.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that the conditional
density <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}=y</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}(u)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V((y+u)/2)-\frac{\|y-u\|^{2}}{8\eta}\Bigr\}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.150em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}(u)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V((y+u)/2)-\frac{\|y-u\|^{2}}{8\eta}\Bigr\}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">and define</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{y}(u)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≔</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.317em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{y}(u)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Differentiating the normalized conditional density gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The negative log-density of <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> has Hessian</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{4}\,\bigl\{\nabla^{2}V((y+u)/2)+\eta^{-1}I\bigr\}\succeq\frac{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}{4}\,I\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{4}\,\bigl\{\nabla^{2}V((y+u)/2)+\eta^{-1}I\bigr\}\succeq\frac{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}{4}\,I\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, the conditional Poincaré inequality implies, for every unit
vector <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\nabla_{u}(a^{\top}s_{y})(Y_{-})\|^{2}\,\bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\bigr]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.317em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{\alpha+\eta^{-1}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\nabla_{u}(a^{\top}s_{y})(Y_{-})\|^{2}\,\bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">The assumptions
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\eta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\eta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> give</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{u}s_{y}(u)\|_{\rm op}\leq\frac{\eta^{-1}-\alpha}{4}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{u}s_{y}(u)\|_{\rm op}\leq\frac{\eta^{-1}-\alpha}{4}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Var\bigl(a^{\top}s_{y}(Y_{-})\bigm|Y_{+}=y\bigr)\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Applying conditional Cauchy–Schwarz in
(<a href="#A3.E1" title="Equation C.1 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) and taking the supremum over
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|a\|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|a\|=1</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)\|^{2}\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)\|^{2}\leq\frac{(\eta^{-1}-\alpha)^{2}}{4\,(\alpha+\eta^{-1})}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">Integrating in <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and using (<a href="#A2.E9" title="Equation B.9 ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{(1-\alpha\eta)^{2}}{4\,(1+\alpha\eta)}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝖴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝖴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{(1-\alpha\eta)^{2}}{4\,(1+\alpha\eta)}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text">Finally,
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which proves (<a href="#A2.E13" title="Equation B.13 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We next bound the spectrum of <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves constants, <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> is centered.
The Poincaré inequality for the marginal law <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{Y}}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)\leq\Bigl(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\eta\Bigr)\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{Y}}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)\leq\Bigl(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\eta\Bigr)\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Combining (<a href="#A3.E3" title="Equation C.3 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>) with
(<a href="#A3.E2" title="Equation C.2 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>) and rearranging gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|\leq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\|f\|=\rho_{\rm mac}\,\|f\|\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|\leq\frac{1-\alpha\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\|f\|=\rho_{\rm mac}\,\|f\|\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is self-adjoint, this proves</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\rho_{\rm mac}I\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\preceq\rho_{\rm mac}I\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\rho_{\rm mac}I\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\preceq\rho_{\rm mac}I\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">It remains only to obtain the uniform lower bound
<math id="A3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\succeq-\frac{1}{2}\,I</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>, exchangeability and
(<a href="#A2.E8" title="Equation B.8 ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.8</span></a>) give</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\bigl(g(Y_{+})-g(Y_{-})\bigr)^{2}\bigr]=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(X+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(X-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[\bigl(g(Y_{+})-g(Y_{-})\bigr)^{2}\bigr]=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{X}\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(X+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(X-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">For fixed <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=x</annotation></semantics></math>, the Gaussian Poincaré inequality implies</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl[\bigl(g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)-g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr)^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl\|\nabla g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)+\nabla g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr\|^{2}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}_{Z}\bigl\|\nabla g(x+\sqrt{\eta}Z)+\nabla g(x-\sqrt{\eta}Z)\bigr\|^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">Averaging in <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and using that <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same
marginal gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla g\|^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\langle g,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})g\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla g\|^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Applying (<a href="#A3.E6" title="Equation C.6 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>) with <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> and using
(<a href="#A2.E13" title="Equation B.13 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>), we obtain</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl\langle f,\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})f\bigr\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl\langle f,\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})f\bigr\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\langle f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})f\bigr\rangle\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>f</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\bigr\rangle\leq 2\eta\,\|\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl\langle f,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})f\bigr\rangle\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, as an operator inequality, <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\preceq I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\preceq I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
or equivalently</span></p>
<table id="A3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}(I+2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\succeq 0\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}(I+2\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\succeq 0\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a self-adjoint contraction, its spectrum lies in
<math id="A3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The scalar inequality corresponding to
(<a href="#A3.E7" title="Equation C.7 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.7</span></a>) is</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\lambda)^{2}(1+2\lambda)\geq 0\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\lambda\in\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\lambda)^{2}(1+2\lambda)\geq 0\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\lambda\in\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">and therefore <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\subseteq[-1/2,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})\subseteq[-1/2,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="A3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{1}{2}I\,\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.428em">⪯</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{1}{2}I\,\preceq\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together with (<a href="#A3.E4" title="Equation C.4 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>), this proves
(<a href="#A2.E14" title="Equation B.14 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.14</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq(1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2})\,I=\frac{4\alpha\eta}{(1+\alpha\eta)^{2}}\,I\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>mac</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq(1-\rho_{\rm mac}^{2})\,I=\frac{4\alpha\eta}{(1+\alpha\eta)^{2}}\,I\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">and therefore</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,I=\gamma_{\rm gap}I\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>gap</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\frac{2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}{1+\alpha\eta}\,I=\gamma_{\rm gap}I\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">This proves (<a href="#A2.E15" title="Equation B.15 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.15</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The half-turn analysis below uses the following alternative form of
(<a href="#A3.E1" title="Equation C.1 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.1</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Derivative formula for <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-almost every <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">First let <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. The representation
(<a href="#A3.E1" title="Equation C.1 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Using the definition of <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{y}(u)=\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\Bigr\}-\nabla V((y+u)/2)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{y}(u)=\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\frac{y-u}{4\eta}\Bigr\}-\nabla V((y+u)/2)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">To rewrite (<a href="#A3.E1" title="Equation C.1 ‣ Proof of . ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) in the claimed form, we
only need to identify the covariance generated by the vector in braces.
Integration by parts in <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> against the conditional density <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\ \Bigm|\ Y_{+}=y\Bigr]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.500em">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\ \Bigm|\ Y_{+}=y\Bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">Applying (<a href="#A3.E9" title="Equation C.9 ‣ Proof. ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.9</span></a>) to <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>, justified by compactly
supported cutoffs converging to <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\Bigl[\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]=0\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" lspace="0.150em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.317em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\Bigl[\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]=0\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">Combining this with (<a href="#A3.E9" title="Equation C.9 ‣ Proof. ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.9</span></a>), we obtain</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Cov}\Bigl(f(Y_{-}),\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr)-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence="false" lspace="0.150em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.317em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Cov}\Bigl(f(Y_{-}),\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr)-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.150em">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.317em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Bigl[f(Y_{-})\,\Bigl\{\frac{1}{2}\nabla V(X)-\frac{y-Y_{-}}{4\eta}\Bigr\}\,\Bigm|\,Y_{+}=y\Bigr]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\nabla f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]-\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">This is (<a href="#A3.E8" title="Equation C.8 ‣ Lemma C.1 (Derivative formula for 𝖴_𝖯𝖯). ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.8</span></a>) for smooth <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The extension to <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard approximation
argument once we check that the covariance term is continuous in
<math id="A3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 2.11 ‣ Item (i) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a>), the conditional
law of <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}=y</annotation></semantics></math> has Poincaré constant at most
<math id="A3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for every unit vector
<math id="A3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Var(a^{\top}\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla^{2}V(X)\,a\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\beta^{2}\eta\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Var(a^{\top}\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)\leq\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla^{2}V(X)\,a\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\beta^{2}\eta\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz and
(<a href="#A2.E9" title="Equation B.9 ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>) therefore imply</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+})\bigr\|_{L^{2}}\leq\beta\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|\mathrm{Cov}(f(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+})\bigr\|_{L^{2}}\leq\beta\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">and the same bound applies to differences. Conditional expectation is
an <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> contraction, and (<a href="#A2.E13" title="Equation B.13 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>) controls the
left side. Thus every term in (<a href="#A3.E8" title="Equation C.8 ‣ Lemma C.1 (Derivative formula for 𝖴_𝖯𝖯). ‣ C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.8</span></a>) is
continuous under <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> approximation. Density of
<math id="A3.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions</h3>

<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition D.1</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hilbert space notation)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\zeta&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\zeta&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Its inner product and
norm are</p>
<table id="A4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\int fg\,\mathrm{d}\zeta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|f\|_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\langle f,f\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}^{1/2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\int fg\,\mathrm{d}\zeta\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|f\|_{L^{2}(\zeta)}\coloneqq\langle f,f\rangle_{L^{2}(\zeta)}^{1/2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We write</p>
<table id="A4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)\coloneqq\Bigl\{f\in L^{2}(\zeta):\int f\,\mathrm{d}\zeta=0\Bigr\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)\coloneqq\Bigl\{f\in L^{2}(\zeta):\int f\,\mathrm{d}\zeta=0\Bigr\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we abbreviate the inner product and norm by
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,g\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,g\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two functions equal <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>-almost everywhere represent the same
vector. A closed linear subspace <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subseteq L^{2}(\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subseteq L^{2}(\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math>
has orthogonal complement
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\perp}\coloneqq\{g:\langle f,g\rangle=0\text{ for all }f\in\mathcal{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\perp}\coloneqq\{g:\langle f,g\rangle=0\text{ for all }f\in\mathcal{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f-\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f-\mathsf{P}_{\mathcal{M}}f\in\mathcal{M}^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators
on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise.
The adjoint <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is characterized by
<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathsf{L}f,g\rangle=\langle f,\mathsf{L}^{*}g\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathsf{L}f,g\rangle=\langle f,\mathsf{L}^{*}g\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
An operator is self-adjoint if <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}=\mathsf{L}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}=\mathsf{L}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contraction
if <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{L}f\|\leq\|f\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{L}f\|\leq\|f\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and unitary if it is onto and preserves
norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{2}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{2}=I</annotation></semantics></math>, is
unitary. For self-adjoint operators <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}_{1},\mathsf{L}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}_{1},\mathsf{L}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{L}_{1}\preceq\mathsf{L}_{2}\hskip 10.00002pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 10.00002pt\langle f,(\mathsf{L}_{2}-\mathsf{L}_{1})f\rangle\geq 0\hskip 10.00002pt\text{for every }f" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⪯</mo><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>f</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>for every </mtext><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}_{1}\preceq\mathsf{L}_{2}\hskip 10.00002pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 10.00002pt\langle f,(\mathsf{L}_{2}-\mathsf{L}_{1})f\rangle\geq 0\hskip 10.00002pt\text{for every }f</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is the Loewner order. If <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is self-adjoint, the
spectral theorem defines <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\mathsf{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\mathsf{L})</annotation></semantics></math> for bounded Borel functions
<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{E}(\mathsf{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{E}(\mathsf{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is the orthogonal
projection onto the spectral set <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and a non-negative operator has
a unique non-negative square root.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition D.2</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Markov operators and density evolution)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be a Markov kernel preserving <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. Its
Markov operator on observables is</p>
<table id="A4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{K}f)(z)\coloneqq\int f(z^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{K}f)(z)\coloneqq\int f(z^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}(z,\mathrm{d}z^{\prime})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is a contraction on <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its adjoint
<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> governs density evolution: if a law has density <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> with
respect to <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, then its density after one step is <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}^{*}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}^{*}h</annotation></semantics></math>.
Constants are fixed by both operators.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> has density <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="A4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi^{2}(P\|\zeta)=\|h-1\|_{\zeta}^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(P\|\zeta)=\|h-1\|_{\zeta}^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, an operator norm bound for <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}^{2}(\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math> gives the same bound for its adjoint
<math id="A4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence gives <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> contraction.</p>
</div>
</section>
